---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import GravelCalculator from '../../components/GravelCalculator.astro';
import { webApplicationSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../utils/schema';
import { CALCULATORS_HUB } from '../../data/tools';

const path = '/calculators/gravel-calculator';
const title = 'Gravel Calculator: Cubic Yards & Tons';
const description =
  'Estimate how much gravel you need in cubic yards and approximate tons. Calculate rectangular, circular, or known-area projects with visible density assumptions.';

const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: CALCULATORS_HUB.title, href: '/calculators' },
  { label: 'Gravel Calculator' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  schema={[
    webApplicationSchema({
      name: 'Gravel Calculator',
      description,
      path,
    }),
    breadcrumbSchema(crumbs),
  ]}
>
  <div class="container-site container-calc calc-page">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />

    <header class="calc-page__intro">
      <h1 class="section-heading">Gravel Calculator</h1>
      <p class="section-lede calc-page__lede">
        Estimate gravel volume in cubic yards and approximate tons for rectangular, circular, or known-area
        projects. Enter the depth you plan to use — this tool does not choose depth for you.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="calc-page__tool">
      <GravelCalculator />
    </div>

    <p class="calc-page__disclaimer">
      This calculator estimates gravel volume from the dimensions and depth you enter. Weight is approximate
      because bulk density varies by material, source, moisture, gradation, and handling. It does not determine
      required project depth, compaction, drainage design, structural suitability, or code compliance. Confirm
      quantities and material requirements with your supplier when needed.
    </p>

    <article class="prose-article calc-page__copy">
      <h2>How the gravel calculation works</h2>
      <p>
        The calculator converts your dimensions to feet, then computes volume in cubic feet. Rectangular projects
        use length × width × depth. Circles use π × radius² × depth. Known-area mode multiplies your area by
        depth. Cubic yards are cubic feet ÷ 27. Extra material is applied as a percentage on top of that calculated
        volume. Approximate weight multiplies volume by the selected loose bulk density (lb/ft³), then converts to
        US tons.
      </p>

      <h2>Cubic yards vs tons</h2>
      <p>
        Cubic yards measure geometric volume — how much space the gravel fills at the depth you enter. Tons measure
        weight, which depends on bulk density. The same cubic yard can weigh differently when the rock type,
        gradation, moisture, or how loosely the material is handled changes. Volume is exact from your inputs;
        tonnage is an estimate from the density assumption shown in the results.
      </p>

      <h2>Gravel density assumptions</h2>
      <p>
        V1 planning densities use Caterpillar earthwork loose-weight figures. They are estimating defaults, not
        supplier guarantees.
      </p>
      <div class="gravel-page__table-wrap">
        <table class="gravel-page__table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Material</th>
              <th scope="col">Density</th>
              <th scope="col">Approx. tons/yd³</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Crushed stone (loose, planning)</td>
              <td>100 lb/ft³</td>
              <td>≈ 1.35</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Dry gravel, ¼–2 in (loose)</td>
              <td>≈ 105.6 lb/ft³</td>
              <td>≈ 1.43</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Custom density</td>
              <td>You enter</td>
              <td>Derived</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Real bulk density varies by quarry, product, moisture, and measurement method. Prefer a density from your
        supplier’s quote or ticket when you have one. Pea gravel and other decorative products are not separate
        presets here because supplier weights vary widely — use Custom density when you have a figure.
      </p>

      <h2>How much extra gravel should you plan for?</h2>
      <p>
        The calculator defaults to 10% extra material as an editable planning allowance for waste and measurement
        variation. It is not a universal requirement, and it does not account for compaction. You can set 0%, 5%,
        10%, or a custom value up to 25%. Dense road-base materials may need separate supplier guidance for
        finished compacted depth versus loose delivery volume.
      </p>

      <h2>Example calculations</h2>
      <h3>10 × 10 ft area, 2 in deep, crushed stone, 10% extra</h3>
      <p>
        Calculated volume ≈ 0.62 yd³ (16.67 ft³). With 10% extra ≈ 0.68 yd³. Approximate purchase weight ≈ 0.9 US
        tons at 100 lb/ft³.
      </p>
      <h3>20 × 30 ft area, 3 in deep, crushed stone, 10% extra</h3>
      <p>
        Calculated volume ≈ 5.56 yd³ (150 ft³). With 10% extra ≈ 6.11 yd³. Approximate purchase weight ≈ 8.3 US tons
        at 100 lb/ft³.
      </p>
      <h3>200 sq ft known area, 4 in deep, 10% extra</h3>
      <p>
        Calculated volume ≈ 2.47 yd³. With 10% extra ≈ 2.72 yd³. Approximate purchase weight ≈ 3.7 US tons at
        100 lb/ft³.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>How do I calculate how much gravel I need?</h3>
      <p>
        Multiply area by depth to get volume, convert to cubic yards (÷ 27 from cubic feet), then optionally
        multiply by bulk density to estimate tons. This calculator performs those steps from your inputs.
      </p>
      <h3>How many tons are in a cubic yard of gravel?</h3>
      <p>
        It depends on bulk density. Using the planning presets here, crushed stone at 100 lb/ft³ is about 1.35 US
        tons per cubic yard, and dry gravel ¼–2 in at about 105.6 lb/ft³ is about 1.43 US tons per cubic yard.
        Supplier products can differ.
      </p>
      <h3>How many square feet does a yard of gravel cover?</h3>
      <p>
        Coverage depends on depth. One cubic yard covers 27 ÷ depth_in_feet square feet. At 2 inches deep
        (1/6 ft), one cubic yard covers about 162 sq ft before extra material.
      </p>
      <h3>Does gravel weight vary by type?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes. Particle size, rock type, fines, moisture, and how loosely the material is handled all change bulk
        density and therefore tons per cubic yard.
      </p>
      <h3>Should I order extra gravel?</h3>
      <p>
        Many estimators add a contingency for uneven grade, spillage, and measurement variance. This tool defaults
        to 10% as an editable planning allowance — not a requirement. Use 0% if you only want the raw geometric
        volume.
      </p>
      <h3>How deep should gravel be?</h3>
      <p>
        Required depth depends on the application (path, decorative bed, driveway base, and so on). This calculator
        does not prescribe depth; enter the depth you already plan to use or that your project guidance specifies.
      </p>
      <h3>Can I use this for pea gravel?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes for volume. For weight, pea gravel bulk density varies by supplier and product, so enter a
        supplier-provided density with Custom when available.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://www.cat.com/en_US/articles/ci-articles/earthwork-volumes-reference-tables.html"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            Caterpillar — Material Density Tables to Help Estimate Earthwork Volumes
          </a>
          — loose crushed stone 2,700 lb/yd³; dry gravel ¼–2 in 2,850 lb/yd³; notes that weight varies with moisture,
          grain size, and compaction
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/publications/research/infrastructure/structures/bridge/15034/003.cfm"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            FHWA HRT-15-034 — Strength Characterization of Open-Graded Aggregates
          </a>
          — measured No. 57 unit-weight range illustrating density variation
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://wsdot.wa.gov/publications/manuals/fulltext/M22-01/620.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            WSDOT Design Manual M 22-01, Chapter 620
          </a>
          — estimating context for aggregate unit weights and stockpile waste allowances (not homeowner design
          requirements)
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .calc-page__copy :where(h2, h3) {
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .gravel-page__table-wrap {
    margin: 1rem 0 1.25rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .gravel-page__table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .gravel-page__table th,
  .gravel-page__table td {
    padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  .gravel-page__table th {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink-subtle);
  }
</style>
